    "knowledge_point": "Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes",
    "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{(x-5)({x}^{3}+1)}{{x}^{2}-4x-5}.$",
    "worked_solution": "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-5)({x}^{3}+1)}{{x}^{2}-4x-5} & =\\frac{(x-5)(x+1)({x}^{2}-x+1)}{(x-5)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-5)(x+1)}({x}^{2}-x+1)}{\\cancel{(x-5)(x+1)}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-1,$ and $x=5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005797/assets/bca412e444ba3f3126cc88394ea466c48c2dcb60916dcf42ffb0abecb2c5ad48.png)",
